1
00:00:00,046 --> 00:00:06,700
When Apple launched the iPhone in 2007, Google was secretly building a phone with a tiny screen and a keyboard.

2
00:00:07,406 --> 00:00:12,772
They took one look at the iPhone, studied how it worked, and rebuilt their whole phone around a touchscreen.

3
00:00:13,710 --> 00:00:19,620
That became Android, and today, it runs on most of the phones on the planet, maybe even the one in your hand.

4
00:00:20,270 --> 00:00:22,000
Google never needed Apple's code.

5
00:00:22,478 --> 00:00:24,920
They just understood how it worked and built their own.

6
00:00:25,614 --> 00:00:28,080
But Google had thousands of engineers to do that.

7
00:00:28,622 --> 00:00:32,480
For people like you and me, the apps we use are still locked boxes.

8
00:00:33,134 --> 00:00:35,640
We can use them, but we can't see how they're built.

9
00:00:36,080 --> 00:00:37,920
And every month, we pay again.

10
00:00:38,734 --> 00:00:41,000
So what if you didn't need a team of engineers?

11
00:00:41,620 --> 00:00:46,040
What if you could look inside any app and build your own version without paying anything?

12
00:00:46,766 --> 00:00:51,820
Two weeks ago, I showed you how to clone any app with Cloud Code, REA, and 9Router.

13
00:00:52,366 --> 00:00:55,748
It worked, but Cloud burned through the credits way too fast.

14
00:00:56,366 --> 00:01:05,840
So today, we're going to explore a free workflow with DeepSeek, REA, and OpenRouter, which lets you use powerful AI models at zero cost per token.

15
00:01:06,414 --> 00:01:11,680
And to prove it works, we'll build free clones of two apps millions of people use every day.

16
00:01:12,238 --> 00:01:13,260
Drop your guess in the comments.

17
00:01:13,902 --> 00:01:16,580
And I've saved the biggest surprise for the end.

18
00:01:17,134 --> 00:01:22,240
People already pay good money for apps like these, and I'll show you how to turn this into a service.

19
00:01:23,054 --> 00:01:24,620
We're doing this in three levels.

20
00:01:25,166 --> 00:01:27,840
In level 1, we'll set up DeepSeek with REA.

21
00:01:28,302 --> 00:01:30,300
In level 2, we'll connect OpenRouter.

22
00:01:30,830 --> 00:01:33,320
And in level 3, we'll build those two clones.

23
00:01:33,710 --> 00:01:35,840
And that's where things get insane.

24
00:01:36,398 --> 00:01:39,020
But before we jump in, give me 30 seconds.

25
00:01:39,630 --> 00:01:43,240
If you're building with AI, the idea is never the hard part.

26
00:01:43,620 --> 00:01:44,480
The setup is.

27
00:01:45,006 --> 00:01:47,160
One missed command, and nothing works.

28
00:01:47,790 --> 00:01:50,100
That's exactly why I built Midas.ai.

29
00:01:50,100 --> 00:01:57,924
Every video I make turns into a step-by-step guide there, with the commands, prompts, and links in order, ready to copy and paste.

30
00:01:58,606 --> 00:02:05,200
The REA setup I showed you two weeks ago is already up, and today's DeepSeq and OpenRouter workflow goes right next to it.

31
00:02:05,742 --> 00:02:12,220
When you're ready to explore more, there are 760 plus AI tools waiting, and you can filter for the free ones.

32
00:02:12,718 --> 00:02:13,460
And the best part?

33
00:02:13,838 --> 00:02:15,100
It's completely free!

34
00:02:15,758 --> 00:02:18,620
So if you're building along with me today, the link's in the description.

35
00:02:21,006 --> 00:02:25,330
And if you're new here, hit subscribe, because we break down stuff like this every day.

36
00:02:25,838 --> 00:02:27,588
All right, let's open the first box.

37
00:02:30,126 --> 00:02:33,820
Before we build anything, we need someone to do the actual building.

38
00:02:34,414 --> 00:02:35,800
And we're not hiring a developer.

39
00:02:36,206 --> 00:02:37,140
We're using one.

40
00:02:37,520 --> 00:02:39,080
It's called DeepSeek Harness.

41
00:02:39,854 --> 00:02:42,380
Think of it as a developer that lives on your computer.

42
00:02:42,990 --> 00:02:49,320
You tell it in plain English what you want, and it writes the code, fixes the errors, and puts everything together for you.

43
00:02:49,902 --> 00:02:51,100
So, let's get it set up!

44
00:02:51,800 --> 00:02:56,612
Search for DeepSeek on Google, open the official website, and click on Harness Desktop.

45
00:02:57,390 --> 00:02:58,640
Download the app for your computer.

46
00:02:59,246 --> 00:03:02,580
I'm on a Mac, so I'll just drag it into Applications and open it.

47
00:03:03,118 --> 00:03:05,092
When it opens, you'll see two options.

48
00:03:05,614 --> 00:03:07,700
Sign in and add a PI key.

49
00:03:08,270 --> 00:03:09,620
We're going with the API key.

50
00:03:10,286 --> 00:03:16,560
Now, an API key sounds technical, but it's really just a password that lets the app talk to DeepSeek's brain.

51
00:03:17,230 --> 00:03:22,620
To get one, search for DeepSeek API, open the DeepSeek platform, and sign in with Google.

52
00:03:23,246 --> 00:03:28,780
Go to API Keys, click Create New API Key, give it a name, and copy it.

53
00:03:29,326 --> 00:03:33,960
Come back to Harness, click Add API Key, paste it in, and continue.

54
00:03:34,606 --> 00:03:36,460
And just like that, we're inside!

55
00:03:37,006 --> 00:03:38,160
Let's take a quick look around.

56
00:03:38,862 --> 00:03:44,100
In Settings, you'll find the Appearance, the Models, Built-in Plugins, and Agent Presets.

57
00:03:44,718 --> 00:03:46,840
We'll use the standard preset for this project.

58
00:03:47,502 --> 00:03:49,120
Now here's the part that matters.

59
00:03:49,760 --> 00:03:52,400
Harness lets you pick which AI model does the thinking.

60
00:03:53,070 --> 00:03:59,844
We've got DeepSeek v4.1 Flash and DeepSeek v4 Pro, and you can even choose how hard it thinks.

61
00:04:00,494 --> 00:04:03,160
For our project, we're setting the thinking effort to high.

62
00:04:03,822 --> 00:04:08,060
But remember, our whole goal is to do this without paying for expensive AI.

63
00:04:08,686 --> 00:04:13,400
So in the next level, we're plugging in OpenRouter, which gives us models we can use for free.

64
00:04:14,094 --> 00:04:17,300
Hold that thought, because right now there's a bigger problem.

65
00:04:17,960 --> 00:04:25,156
Our developer is smart, but if we just say, build me a copy of this app, it has no idea how the original actually works.

66
00:04:25,582 --> 00:04:26,400
It would be guessing.

67
00:04:27,022 --> 00:04:32,080
And guessing is how you end up with something that looks right, but falls apart the moment you click a button.

68
00:04:32,782 --> 00:04:34,400
That's where REA comes in.

69
00:04:35,020 --> 00:04:37,700
REA stands for reverse engineer anything.

70
00:04:38,414 --> 00:04:40,980
Think of it as a detective's toolkit for software.

71
00:04:41,640 --> 00:04:44,420
On its own, it's just a box of tools sitting on a shelf.

72
00:04:44,910 --> 00:04:48,260
But plug it into your AI, and the AI becomes the detective.

73
00:04:48,974 --> 00:04:53,828
It can open up an app, study how it's built, and trace exactly how each feature works.

74
00:04:54,478 --> 00:04:57,840
Desktop apps, Electron apps, even compiled programs.

75
00:04:58,340 --> 00:05:00,160
REA has a tool for each of them.

76
00:05:00,782 --> 00:05:05,668
So instead of guessing, our developer actually investigates first, and then builds.

77
00:05:05,902 --> 00:05:07,860
And REA isn't tied to one AI.

78
00:05:08,300 --> 00:05:13,460
It works with Cloud Code, Gemini, CLI, Cursor, and a bunch of others.

79
00:05:13,934 --> 00:05:16,360
Today, we're plugging it into DeepSeek Harness.

80
00:05:17,038 --> 00:05:28,400
By the way, if you want the full deep dive on REA, we've already made a complete video on it called You Can Clone Any App For Free – REA plus 9 Router, a complete end-to-end tutorial.

81
00:05:28,910 --> 00:05:30,020
The link is in the description.

82
00:05:30,670 --> 00:05:33,040
Now, let's plug the toolkit into our developer.

83
00:05:33,040 --> 00:05:36,740
Search for REA on Google and open its GitHub page.

84
00:05:37,326 --> 00:05:42,000
Scroll down to the README where you'll see its features, supported apps, and the install instructions.

85
00:05:42,660 --> 00:05:51,460
Open your terminal and paste this one line, npx rea-agents at latest setup dash dash client dsh.

86
00:05:52,014 --> 00:05:52,500
Hit enter.

87
00:05:53,060 --> 00:05:56,900
That one line tells REA, set yourself up for DeepSeek Harness.

88
00:05:57,582 --> 00:05:59,780
You'll see a short plan of what it's about to install.

89
00:06:00,238 --> 00:06:06,160
In our that's the REA skill, the harness settings, and an extra analysis tool called Hopper.

90
00:06:06,638 --> 00:06:10,220
Think of Hopper as a magnifying glass for apps that are harder to read.

91
00:06:10,720 --> 00:06:12,160
Type Y and let it run.

92
00:06:12,654 --> 00:06:18,420
Once it's done, we need to make sure everything actually works, and REA has its own health check for that.

93
00:06:18,766 --> 00:06:20,620
They literally call it the doctor.

94
00:06:21,358 --> 00:06:24,100
npx rea-agents doctor.

95
00:06:24,654 --> 00:06:28,940
The doctor checks every tool, every connection, and tells you if anything is missing.

96
00:06:29,518 --> 00:06:30,720
You want it all green.

97
00:06:31,220 --> 00:06:33,412
And if something isn't green, here's the trick.

98
00:06:33,920 --> 00:06:35,260
Don't try to fix it yourself.

99
00:06:35,780 --> 00:06:39,172
Copy the error, paste it into Harness, and say, fix this.

100
00:06:39,758 --> 00:06:42,560
That's how you do technical setups without being technical.

101
00:06:43,118 --> 00:06:45,340
Last step, restart DeepSeek Harness.

102
00:06:45,966 --> 00:06:49,780
Just like your phone after an update, it loads its new tools when it starts up again.

103
00:06:50,318 --> 00:06:53,040
So, in plain English, here's what we've got so far.

104
00:06:53,486 --> 00:06:56,560
DeepSeek Harness is our developer, the one who writes the code.

105
00:06:57,040 --> 00:07:00,740
REA is the detectives toolkit that lets it look inside other apps.

106
00:07:01,230 --> 00:07:03,200
And the doctor makes sure it's all connected.

107
00:07:03,820 --> 00:07:12,860
And if you've made it this far with your setup working, quick favor, hit subscribe, because we break down tools like this every single day, and you don't want to miss the next one.

108
00:07:13,294 --> 00:07:15,140
But there's still one piece missing.

109
00:07:15,630 --> 00:07:20,140
Our developer needs a brain to think with, and we don't want to pay for an expensive one.

110
00:07:20,590 --> 00:07:22,440
That's exactly what we're fixing next.

111
00:07:22,702 --> 00:07:25,240
And this is where the free AI models come in.

112
00:07:25,326 --> 00:07:25,940
Level 2.

113
00:07:26,510 --> 00:07:27,680
Setting up OpenRouter.

114
00:07:28,462 --> 00:07:36,020
So now we've got our developer, DeepSeek Harness, and we've given it REA, the most important toolkit it needs to study other apps.

115
00:07:36,590 --> 00:07:38,980
But there's still one thing missing in this setup.

116
00:07:39,598 --> 00:07:42,420
We need an AI model to actually power our developer.

117
00:07:43,086 --> 00:07:45,540
And we don't want to keep paying for every little task.

118
00:07:46,158 --> 00:07:47,820
That's where OpenRouter comes in.

119
00:07:48,430 --> 00:07:52,900
Think of OpenRouter as a place where you can access different AI models through one connection.

120
00:07:52,910 --> 00:07:54,080
And the best part?

121
00:07:54,574 --> 00:07:56,480
Some of these models are free to use.

122
00:07:57,038 --> 00:07:58,480
So let's connect it to our setup.

123
00:07:59,246 --> 00:08:04,120
First, open OpenRouter.ai, click Sign Up, and continue with Google.

124
00:08:04,814 --> 00:08:08,320
Select your account, complete the verification, and choose Individual.

125
00:08:09,220 --> 00:08:11,580
Now OpenRouter will give you an API key.

126
00:08:12,200 --> 00:08:15,200
Copy it and keep it safe, and don't share it with anyone.

127
00:08:15,566 --> 00:08:16,440
We'll need it in a second.

128
00:08:17,166 --> 00:08:22,160
You'll also see an option to add a payment method, but since we're using free models, we'll skip that for now.

129
00:08:22,766 --> 00:08:24,540
And just like that, we're inside.

130
00:08:25,166 --> 00:08:27,076
Now let's head back to DeepSeq Harness.

131
00:08:27,720 --> 00:08:31,240
Open Settings, go to Models, and add a custom provider.

132
00:08:31,854 --> 00:08:33,380
We'll call it OpenRouterFree.

133
00:08:34,094 --> 00:08:35,844
For the base URL, paste this.

134
00:08:36,590 --> 00:08:38,720
Then add the API key we just copied.

135
00:08:39,278 --> 00:08:39,880
And there we go!

136
00:08:40,206 --> 00:08:42,300
Our developer can now connect to OpenRouter.

137
00:08:42,958 --> 00:08:46,880
But before we move ahead, let's quickly check whether REA is working properly.

138
