00:15:03,086 --> 00:15:06,240
There are even message reactions and a separate panel for replies.

221
00:15:06,926 --> 00:15:09,100
It actually looks pretty close to what we wanted.

222
00:15:09,806 --> 00:15:11,236
But here's the real test.

223
00:15:11,950 --> 00:15:12,480
Does it work?

224
00:15:12,580 --> 00:15:13,280
Let's find out!

225
00:15:13,806 --> 00:15:15,900
First, let's click on the engineering channel.

226
00:15:16,526 --> 00:15:17,480
And there we go!

227
00:15:17,806 --> 00:15:21,240
The conversation changes and we can see messages from a different team.

228
00:15:21,870 --> 00:15:23,560
Now let's create our own channel.

229
00:15:24,160 --> 00:15:28,120
Click Add Channels, type Quarterly Planning, and hit Create.

230
00:15:28,622 --> 00:15:30,840
And just like that, our new channel appears.

231
00:15:31,566 --> 00:15:35,100
Let's send a message asking the team to share their priorities for next quarter.

232
00:15:35,694 --> 00:15:36,560
And there it is!

233
00:15:36,782 --> 00:15:39,160
Our message appears, along with a sample reply.

234
00:15:39,758 --> 00:15:45,420
We can even reply to a specific message by clicking the Reply icon, which opens a separate thread on the right.

235
00:15:46,030 --> 00:15:47,364
Now, let's try something else.

236
00:15:47,880 --> 00:15:53,300
I'll attach meeting-notes.txt, add a short message, and send it.

237
00:15:53,742 --> 00:15:54,480
And there we go!

238
00:15:54,798 --> 00:15:58,660
The file appears in our conversation, and we can find it in the Files tab, too.

239
00:15:59,360 --> 00:16:07,400
Moving on, we can also send direct messages to teammates, open a huddle for audio conversations, and search for channels like Quarterly Planning.

240
00:16:07,982 --> 00:16:12,164
There's even a Notes tab where we can keep track of project priorities and next steps.

241
00:16:12,878 --> 00:16:15,080
So, quite a few features are working already.

242
00:16:15,680 --> 00:16:19,748
And just to make sure this wasn't a one-time thing, we ran another quick test.

243
00:16:20,270 --> 00:16:28,180
We created another channel called Launch Planning, sent a message, attached a file, opened a thread, and tested Search and Huddle again.

244
00:16:28,654 --> 00:16:30,960
And the main interactions worked this time, too!

245
00:16:31,630 --> 00:16:34,380
Now, of course, this isn't the complete Slack app.

246
00:16:34,670 --> 00:16:39,380
It's a local version with sample conversations, not a fully connected messaging platform.

247
00:16:39,982 --> 00:16:41,620
But think about what we've just done.

248
00:16:42,140 --> 00:16:49,560
We started with a prompt, let DeepSeek handle the building process, and ended up with an app where we can actually interact with different features.

249
00:16:50,190 --> 00:16:53,240
And we did it using the free AI models we connected earlier.

250
00:16:53,934 --> 00:16:56,320
Now, Slack was just our first challenge.

251
00:16:56,750 --> 00:17:00,644
We've got another app to recreate, and this one's going to be just as interesting.

252
00:17:01,326 --> 00:17:03,680
So let's see how our setup handles the next one.

253
00:17:03,854 --> 00:17:07,100
And this time, we're taking on something a little more challenging.

254
00:17:07,100 --> 00:17:10,740
We're going to build our own version of ChatGPT.

255
00:17:11,374 --> 00:17:13,420
Now, we all know ChatGPT.

256
00:17:13,934 --> 00:17:18,640
You type a question, it gives you an answer, and you can have entire conversations with it.

257
00:17:19,182 --> 00:17:22,180
But there's a lot more happening behind that simple chat box.

258
00:17:22,830 --> 00:17:33,540
So, for our second challenge, we're going to see if DeepSeek can help us build something similar with a clean chat interface, conversation history, and all the features that make it easy to use.

259
00:17:34,094 --> 00:17:34,740
And the best part?

260
00:17:35,214 --> 00:17:37,040
We don't need to set everything up again.

261
00:17:37,518 --> 00:17:42,680
We're using the same DeepSeek Harness, REA, and OpenRouter setup that we used to build Slack.

262
00:17:43,182 --> 00:17:44,380
So, let's get started!

263
00:17:45,038 --> 00:17:50,052
Just like we did earlier, open DeepSeek Harness and make sure our OpenRouter preset is selected.

264
00:17:50,862 --> 00:17:54,880
Now, remember how we gave DeepSeek a detailed prompt to build our Slack workspace?

265
00:17:55,662 --> 00:18:08,560
We're going to do the same thing here, but this time, we're asking it to build a chat GPT-style application, clean chat interface, conversation history, a message box, and a responsive layout that works on different screen sizes.

266
00:18:09,230 --> 00:18:12,720
And I'll leave the complete prompt in the description so you can try it yourself!

267
00:18:13,400 --> 00:18:20,180
Now DeepSeek starts checking our workspace and REA connection, planning the project, and creating the necessary files.

268
00:18:20,814 --> 00:18:26,220
It builds the sidebar, message input, and chat interface without us having to write the code ourselves.

269
00:18:26,830 --> 00:18:29,660
Once it's ready, I ran it in the browser to see what we'd built.

270
00:18:30,140 --> 00:18:30,980
And look at this!

271
00:18:31,342 --> 00:18:38,700
We've got our own chat-GPT style app, with a sidebar, a new chat button, conversation history, and a message box.

272
00:18:39,214 --> 00:18:41,100
But let's see what it can actually do.

273
00:18:41,710 --> 00:18:45,720
First, let's ask it to explain photosynthesis in three simple steps.

274
00:18:46,286 --> 00:18:48,480
And here's where things get even more interesting.

275
00:18:49,102 --> 00:18:54,860
We can generate both images and videos using just a simple text prompt right inside our chatbot.

276
00:18:54,860 --> 00:18:59,260
For example, let's ask it to create a peaceful sunset over a lake.

277
00:18:59,800 --> 00:19:00,580
And there we go!

278
00:19:00,974 --> 00:19:03,160
It generates the image based on our description.

279
00:19:03,918 --> 00:19:05,880
We can even create videos the same way.

280
00:19:06,382 --> 00:19:09,508
Just describe what we want, and the AI can generate it for us.

281
00:19:10,062 --> 00:19:10,800
And the best part?

282
00:19:11,310 --> 00:19:15,140
All our conversations are saved in the sidebar, so we can come back to them anytime.

283
00:19:15,982 --> 00:19:17,780
Now let's explore some more features.

284
00:19:18,510 --> 00:19:22,360
Click the plus icon beside the message box, and you'll see quite a few options.

285
00:19:22,360 --> 00:19:27,720
First is search the web, followed by deep research, and analyze a URL.

286
00:19:28,494 --> 00:19:36,020
Then we've got create an image, create a video, add photos and files, my media, and upload images or videos.

287
00:19:36,366 --> 00:19:38,520
Let's quickly see what some of these actually do.

288
00:19:39,086 --> 00:19:44,260
We'll start with search the web and paste a Wikipedia link about news television channels around the world.

289
00:19:44,820 --> 00:19:45,700
And look at that!

290
00:19:46,062 --> 00:19:53,028
It organizes the channels by region, – including Asia, Europe, Africa, and the Americas – along with source links.

291
00:19:53,680 --> 00:19:56,500
Now, using the same link, let's try Deep Research.

292
00:19:57,230 --> 00:20:03,240
This time, it gives us a more detailed report, looking at the reliability of the information and where there might be gaps.

293
00:20:03,886 --> 00:20:06,140
Next, let's try Analyze a URL.

294
00:20:06,894 --> 00:20:11,880
And here, we get a shorter summary of the webpage, covering its main points and important details.

295
00:20:12,558 --> 00:20:16,860
So, instead of reading through the entire page, we can quickly understand what it's about.

296
00:20:17,500 --> 00:20:18,860
Now, moving on to media.

297
00:20:19,440 --> 00:20:23,740
We can upload a reference image, add a prompt, and use the image creation option.

298
00:20:24,430 --> 00:20:29,252
There's also My Media, where our uploaded pictures and videos are organized in one place.

299
00:20:29,620 --> 00:20:33,508
And with Create a Video, we can enter a description of the video we want to make.

300
00:20:34,094 --> 00:20:41,440
Now, the image and video creation options are part of our interface, although we haven't demonstrated completed AI-generated media here.

301
00:20:41,998 --> 00:20:43,280
But look at what we've built!

302
00:20:43,822 --> 00:20:52,200
We can have AI conversations, save our chat history, search the web, get research reports, summarize webpages, and manage media.

303
00:20:52,878 --> 00:20:58,460
Of course, this isn't a complete replacement for ChatGPT, and some features still need further testing.

304
00:20:59,118 --> 00:21:06,820
But we started by building our own Slack workspace, and now we've created a ChatGPT-style app using the exact same setup.

305
00:21:07,300 --> 00:21:11,700
Two completely different applications built without manually writing the code.

306
00:21:12,270 --> 00:21:14,628
And that's what makes this workflow so interesting.

307
00:21:15,310 --> 00:21:20,980
You can start with an idea, turn it into something you can actually use, and keep improving it as you go.

308
00:21:22,574 --> 00:21:23,980
And that's all three levels!

309
00:21:24,526 --> 00:21:31,220
We started with DeepSeek Harness, connected REA, and used OpenRouter to power everything with free AI models.

310
00:21:31,726 --> 00:21:40,500
Then we put the entire setup to the test by building our own versions of Slack and ChatGPT without writing the code ourselves or needing a paid AI subscription.

311
00:21:40,500 --> 00:21:41,860
And the best part?

312
00:21:42,318 --> 00:21:43,940
You're not limited to these two apps.

313
00:21:44,320 --> 00:21:50,980
You can take an idea, describe what you want in plain English, and let these models help you turn it into something that actually works.

314
00:21:51,380 --> 00:21:54,760
And all the prompts and resources from today's video are in the description, too.

315
00:21:55,182 --> 00:22:01,160
Now, if you want to stop just hearing about AI and actually start building things with it, subscribe to Midas.

316
00:22:01,550 --> 00:22:08,840
We're constantly testing free AI tools, finding alternatives to expensive software, and sharing practical workflows that you can use yourself.

317
00:22:09,294 --> 00:22:11,972
And tell me in the comments, which app would you build first?

